**Current Requirements:**
```
SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-based Access Control (RBAC) system operating in a multi-user environment with support for role hierarchy, temporary delegation of roles, revocation of delegated roles, and concurrent request processing. Users have security clearance levels (High, Medium, Low) which constrain their permissions and delegation capabilities. Requests affecting system objects are classified by the highest classification of affected objects, and can only be submitted if the user's clearance permits. Higher-clearance users can approve or reject lower-clearance users' requests. Requests are processed concurrently but normally in FIFO order, with guaranteed eventual processing. Temporary role delegation is allowed from higher-clearance users to lower-clearance users if minimum clearance requirements are met. Delegated roles expire after a fixed time or can be revoked anytime by administrators.

A new Emergency Module is to be integrated, adding functionality to handle emergency scenarios with special modes, privileges, and behavior changes.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
==========================================
- Users have one of three clearance levels: High, Medium, or Low.
- Administrators are users with High clearance, but not all High-clearance users are administrators.
- Users may hold assigned roles and delegated roles; delegation is constrained by clearance.
- Requests affect one or more system objects with classification levels (High, Medium, Low).
- Request classification is the highest classification level of any affected object.
- Users may only submit requests with classification ≤ user clearance.
- Higher-clearance users can approve or reject requests from lower-clearance users.
- Requests are processed concurrently in FIFO order.
- Every request is eventually processed (liveness).
- Role delegation is temporary and may be revoked by administrators.
- Delegated roles must expire after a fixed duration or via revocation.
- The system preserves authorization correctness, delegation and permission consistency, liveness of request processing, and safety under concurrency.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
=======================
R1: Emergency Triggering
While in Normal mode, exactly two administrators must trigger an emergency simultaneously to cause the system to enter Emergency mode.

R2: Emergency Mode Duration and Recovery
Emergency mode is active only for a limited time, after which the system must eventually return to Normal mode.

R3: Emergency Mode Behavior
R3.1: Request Processing Priority
During Emergency mode, request processing may bypass FIFO ordering to prioritize processing multiple high-classification requests concurrently.

R3.2: Mutual Exclusivity of Roles
During Emergency mode, users may temporarily hold roles that are mutually exclusive under normal operation.

R3.3: Emergency Privileges for Administrators
Administrators hold additional emergency privileges temporarily while in Emergency mode.

R4: Post-Emergency Actions
R4.1: Revocation of Emergency Privileges
When Emergency mode ends, emergency privileges must be revoked from administrators.

R4.2: Revocation of Delegated Roles
Delegated roles granted during Emergency mode must be revoked after the mode ends.

R4.3: Administrator Credential Update
Administrators must update their credentials after Emergency mode ends.

KEY CONCEPTS AND ENTITIES
==========================
- User: An entity with a clearance level who holds assigned and delegated roles and can submit requests.
- Administrator: A High-clearance user with special privileges and authority.
- Clearance Level: One of {High, Medium, Low} assigned to users and system objects.
- Role: A set of permissions; roles may have minimum clearance requirements.
- Delegated Role: A role temporarily granted to a user by a higher-clearance user.
- Request: An action submitted by a user affecting one or more system objects.
- System Object: An entity affected by requests, classified by clearance level.
- Request Classification: The highest classification of all affected objects in a request.
- Emergency Mode: A special system mode triggered by two administrators with altered processing rules and privileges.
- Emergency Privileges: Temporary privileges granted to administrators only during Emergency mode; modeled as distinct permissions.
- Mutual Exclusive Roles (during Emergency mode): Roles that normally cannot be held simultaneously by the same user but may be temporarily held during Emergency mode; modeled as pairs of mutually exclusive roles whose exclusivity is relaxed during Emergency.

ASSUMPTIONS REQUIRING CLARIFICATION
====================================
1. In requirement R1, does "exactly two administrators" mean that any pair of two administrators triggering an emergency simultaneously suffice, or must it be a specific pair? Also, what is the temporal window defining "simultaneously"?

2. For R3.2, what are the specific mutual exclusivity constraints on roles during normal operation, and which roles can be held simultaneously during Emergency mode? Is this mutual exclusivity completely lifted or selectively overridden?

3. Regarding R3.3 and R4.3, what constitutes "emergency privileges" and "credential updates" for administrators? Are these modeled as roles, permissions, or special attributes?

4. In R4.2, are only delegated roles granted during the Emergency mode revoked, or do existing delegated roles from Normal mode persist?

5. Is the limited time duration of Emergency mode (R2) fixed or variable, and is it externally controlled or internally managed? Are there constraints on the timing of mode transitions?

6. For the request processing changes in R3.1, is prioritization limited strictly to high-classification requests, or can requests of other classifications also be prioritized? Is concurrency increased only for high-classification requests?

CLARIFICATIONS INCORPORATED
==========================
1. Any two administrators may trigger the emergency concurrently to enter Emergency mode. "Simultaneously" means within a defined short temporal window (abstracted as a concurrency event).

2. Mutual exclusivity is abstracted as different pairs of roles that cannot be held simultaneously under normal operation. During Emergency mode, this mutual exclusivity is completely lifted, allowing users to hold any pair of mutually exclusive roles temporarily.

3. Emergency privileges are abstracted as distinct permissions granted only to administrators during Emergency mode.

4. Delegated role revocation after Emergency mode applies only to roles delegated during Emergency. Delegated roles granted prior to Emergency persist during Emergency unless expired or explicitly revoked.

5. Emergency mode duration is fixed but can be ended earlier manually by internal system control. The timing of mode transitions is internally managed with constraints ensuring eventual return to Normal mode.

6. Request processing priority ordering during Emergency mode is modeled as High > Medium > Low. Concurrency applies to all classes, meaning all High-classification requests should be processed concurrently first, then Medium, then Low requests, bypassing FIFO ordering accordingly.

CONSTRAINTS
===========
- User clearance levels and object classification levels are from the set {High, Medium, Low}, with ordering High > Medium > Low.
- Users may submit requests only if request classification ≤ user clearance.
- Delegation of roles requires the delegate to meet the role’s minimum clearance.
- Delegated roles expire after a fixed time or may be revoked by administrators.
- Requests are processed concurrently in FIFO order during Normal mode.
- Every submitted request is eventually processed.
- Emergency mode can only be entered from Normal mode when exactly two administrators trigger simultaneously (any pair, within a short temporal concurrency window).
- Emergency mode duration is fixed and must eventually return to Normal mode but can be ended earlier manually.
- During Emergency mode, FIFO order may be bypassed to prioritize request processing by classification level: High > Medium > Low.
- Concurrency during Emergency mode applies to all request classes; all requests of a given classification are processed concurrently before processing requests of the next lower classification.
- Mutual exclusivity constraints on roles are completely relaxed during Emergency mode, allowing users to hold mutually exclusive pairs of roles temporarily.
- Emergency privileges are distinct permissions granted to administrators only during Emergency mode and revoked afterwards.
- Delegated roles granted during Emergency mode are revoked when Emergency mode ends; delegated roles granted before Emergency persist during Emergency unless expired or explicitly revoked.
- Administrators must update credentials after Emergency mode ends.
```

**[LESSON]:** Explicitly abstracting temporal simultaneity as concurrency events enables clear, verifiable modeling of triggers requiring simultaneous actions. Modeling emergency privileges as separate permission sets and relaxing mutual exclusivity as a temporary override simplifies reasoning about exceptional modes.

**[PATTERN]:** Temporarily lifting normally strict constraints (like mutual exclusivity or FIFO ordering) during emergency modes is a common pattern, necessitating clear temporal scoping and revocation rules to restore normal operation.

**[EVENT]:** Incorporated user clarifications by adding explicit definitions of concurrency window for emergency triggering, mutual exclusivity relaxation, emergency privileges as permissions, fixed but manually endable emergency duration, and priority-based concurrent request processing during Emergency mode.


**Current Alloy Model:**
```alloy
// Alloy 6 model of RBAC system with Emergency Module per given requirements

open util/ordering[Time] // for linear time modeling

//////////////////////////////
// Basic sets and ordering //
//////////////////////////////

abstract sig ClearanceLevel {}
one sig High, Medium, Low extends ClearanceLevel {}

fact ClearanceOrder {
  High != Medium && Medium != Low && High != Low
}

// Define a total order High > Medium > Low for clearance comparison
pred clearanceLeq[c1,c2: ClearanceLevel] {
  (c1 = Low) => (c2 = Low || c2 = Medium || c2 = High)
  (c1 = Medium) => (c2 = Medium || c2 = High)
  (c1 = High) => (c2 = High)
}

pred clearanceLess[c1,c2: ClearanceLevel] {
  clearanceLeq[c1,c2] && c1 != c2
}

/////////////////////////
// Roles and Permissions//
/////////////////////////

sig Permission {} // abstract permissions including emergency privileges

sig Role {
  minClearance: one ClearanceLevel,
  permissions: set Permission
}

/////////////////////////
// Users and Administrators //
/////////////////////////

sig User {
  clearance: one ClearanceLevel,
  assignedRoles: set Role,
  delegatedRoles: set DelegatedRole,
  updatedCredentialsAfterEmergency: set Time // times when credentials updated after emergency
}

sig Administrator in User {} // subset of User: high clearance & admins

fact AdminClearance {
  all a: Administrator | a.clearance = High
  // Not all high clearance users are admins:
  some u: User | u.clearance = High && u not in Administrator
}

////////////////////
// Delegations //
////////////////////

sig DelegatedRole {
  role: one Role,
  delegatedBy: one User, // must have higher clearance
  delegatedTo: one User,
  delegatedAt: one Time,
  expiresAt: one Time,
  revokedAt: lone Time // optional revocation time; none means not revoked
}

////////////////////
// System Objects //
////////////////////

sig SysObject {
  classification: one ClearanceLevel
}

////////////////////
// Requests //
////////////////////

sig Request {
  submittedBy: one User,
  affectedObjects: set SysObject,
  submittedAt: one Time,
  processedAt: lone Time, // none means not processed yet
  status: one RequestStatus
}

abstract sig RequestStatus {}
one sig Pending, Approved, Rejected extends RequestStatus {}

/////////////////////
// Request Classification //
/////////////////////

fun requestClassification[r: Request]: ClearanceLevel {
  // highest classification among affected objects
  let cls = r.affectedObjects.classification |
  if High in cls then High
  else if Medium in cls then Medium
  else Low
}

/////////////////////
// Emergency Mode //
/////////////////////

// System mode at each time point
abstract sig SystemMode {}
one sig Normal, Emergency extends SystemMode {}

// Emergency triggering event: a concurrency event of exactly two admins triggering emergency
sig EmergencyTriggerEvent {
  triggers: set Administrator, // exactly two admins
  occursAt: one Time
}

fact EmergencyTriggerEventCardinality {
  all ete: EmergencyTriggerEvent | #ete.triggers = 2
}

/////////////////////
// Mutual Exclusion of Roles //
/////////////////////

sig MutualExclusivePair {
  r1, r2: one Role
}

fact MutualExclusiveDistinct {
  all mep: MutualExclusivePair | mep.r1 != mep.r2
}

///////////////////////
// Emergency Privileges //
///////////////////////

sig EmergencyPrivilege extends Permission {}

///////////////////////
// Global Time and Mode //
///////////////////////

sig Time {}

one sig TimeOrdering {
  first: one Time,
  next: Time -> lone Time
}

///////////////////////
// Temporal States //
///////////////////////

// We model system state as a mapping from Time to system mode, requests, delegations, user roles, etc.

// For mutable info per time, we use var fields or relations indexed by Time

///////////////////////////
// System state per Time  //
///////////////////////////

sig State {
  time: one Time,
  mode: one SystemMode,
  activeDelegations: set DelegatedRole, // delegations active at this time (not expired or revoked)
  processedRequests: set Request, // requests processed at or before this time
  emergencyTriggers: set EmergencyTriggerEvent, // triggers occurred at or before this time
  users: set User,
  // user roles at time: assignedRoles are static, delegatedRoles change
  delegatedRolesAt: User -> set Role, // roles delegated and active (from activeDelegations)
  emergencyPrivilegesAt: set Permission, // emergency privileges granted now
  credentialsUpdatedAfterEmergencyAt: User -> set Time // per user, times of credential update after emergency ended
}

///////////////////////////
// Facts about clearance //
///////////////////////////

fact ClearanceTotalOrder {
  // For modeling clearance comparisons, define ordering relations:
  // Use helper predicates clearanceLeq and clearanceLess above
  all c: ClearanceLevel | clearanceLeq[c,c]
}

///////////////////////////
// Users and roles facts //
///////////////////////////

fact UserRolesContainment {
  all s: State, u: User |
    s.delegatedRolesAt[u] = { dr: DelegatedRole | dr.delegatedTo = u && dr in s.activeDelegations } .role
}

///////////////////////////
// Delegation constraints //
///////////////////////////

fact DelegationClearanceConstraint {
  all s: State, dr: DelegatedRole |
    dr in s.activeDelegations =>
      clearanceLess[dr.delegatedBy.clearance, dr.delegatedTo.clearance] = false and
      clearanceLeq[dr.role.minClearance, dr.delegatedTo.clearance]
}

///////////////////////////
// Delegation expiry and revocation //
///////////////////////////

fact DelegationActiveIfNotExpiredOrRevoked {
  all s: State, dr: DelegatedRole |
    dr in s.activeDelegations =>
      dr.delegatedAt in Time and dr.expiresAt in Time and
      // s.time between delegatedAt and expiresAt and before revokedAt if any
      let t = s.time |
        (dr.delegatedAt <= t and t < dr.expiresAt) and
        (no dr.revokedAt or t < dr.revokedAt)
}

///////////////////////////
// Request submission constraints //
///////////////////////////

fact RequestSubmissionClearance {
  all s: State, r: Request |
    r.submittedAt = s.time =>
      clearanceLeq[requestClassification[r], r.submittedBy.clearance]
}

///////////////////////////
// Request processing FIFO and concurrency //
///////////////////////////

fact RequestProcessingNormalModeFIFO {
  all s: State |
    s.mode = Normal =>
      // Requests processed in FIFO order
      all r1, r2: Request |
        r1 in s.processedRequests && r2 in s.processedRequests &&
        r1.submittedAt < r2.submittedAt =>
        r1.processedAt <= r2.processedAt
}

///////////////////////////
// Request processing Emergency mode priority and concurrency //
///////////////////////////

fact RequestProcessingEmergencyPriority {
  all s: State |
    s.mode = Emergency =>
      // Requests processed in priority order by classification: High > Medium > Low
      // All requests of higher priority must be processed before any of lower priority
      all r1, r2: Request |
        r1 in s.processedRequests && r2 in s.processedRequests &&
        let c1 = requestClassification[r1], c2 = requestClassification[r2] |
          clearanceLess[c2, c1] => // c1 > c2
          r2.processedAt > r1.processedAt
}

///////////////////////////
// Mutual exclusivity of roles //
///////////////////////////

fact MutualExclusivityNormal {
  all s: State, u: User |
    s.mode = Normal =>
      // user cannot hold any pair of mutually exclusive roles simultaneously
      all mep: MutualExclusivePair |
        not (mep.r1 in u.assignedRoles + s.delegatedRolesAt[u] &&
             mep.r2 in u.assignedRoles + s.delegatedRolesAt[u])
}

fact MutualExclusivityRelaxedEmergency {
  all s: State |
    s.mode = Emergency =>
      // mutual exclusivity constraints are lifted; no restriction here
      // So no fact forbidding it
      true
}

///////////////////////////
// Emergency privileges //
///////////////////////////

fact EmergencyPrivilegesGranted {
  all s: State |
    s.mode = Emergency =>
      s.emergencyPrivilegesAt = (Administrator & s.users).permissions & EmergencyPrivilege
}

fact EmergencyPrivilegesRevokedAfter {
  all s1, s2: State |
    s1.mode = Emergency && s2.mode = Normal && s1.time < s2.time =>
      no p: EmergencyPrivilege | p in s2.emergencyPrivilegesAt
}

///////////////////////////
// Delegated roles during Emergency //
///////////////////////////

fact DelegatedRoleRevocationAfterEmergency {
  all s1, s2: State |
    s1.mode = Emergency && s2.mode = Normal && s1.time < s2.time =>
      // Delegated roles granted during Emergency are revoked after Emergency ends
      all dr: DelegatedRole |
        (dr.delegatedAt in (s1.time..s2.time) && dr in s1.activeDelegations) =>
          dr not in s2.activeDelegations
}

///////////////////////////
// Credential update after Emergency //
///////////////////////////

fact AdminCredentialUpdateAfterEmergency {
  all s: State |
    s.mode = Normal =>
      all a: Administrator & s.users |
        some t: Time | t > s.time && t in a.updatedCredentialsAfterEmergency
}

///////////////////////////
// Emergency triggering //
///////////////////////////

fact EmergencyTriggeredByTwoAdminsConcurrently {
  // Emergency mode only entered if exactly two admins triggered emergency concurrently
  all s1, s2: State |
    s1.mode = Normal && s2.mode = Emergency && s1.time < s2.time =>
      some ete: EmergencyTriggerEvent |
        ete.occursAt = s2.time &&
        #ete.triggers = 2 &&
        all a: ete.triggers | a in Administrator
}

///////////////////////////
// Emergency mode duration and recovery //
///////////////////////////

fact EmergencyDurationFixedAndEventuallyEnds {
  // Emergency mode active for fixed duration but can be ended earlier manually
  // Model with facts ensuring eventually recovery to Normal mode
  all s: State |
    s.mode = Emergency =>
      some s2: State | s2.mode = Normal && s2.time > s.time
}

///////////////////////////
// Delegation clearance ordering //
// Delegate must have clearance >= role minClearance and delegator must have higher clearance than delegate
///////////////////////////

fact DelegationClearanceOrdering {
  all dr: DelegatedRole |
    clearanceLess[dr.delegatedBy.clearance, dr.delegatedTo.clearance] = false and
    clearanceLeq[dr.role.minClearance, dr.delegatedTo.clearance]
}

///////////////////////////
// Run predicates for incremental verification //
///////////////////////////

pred R1 {
  // R1: Emergency Triggering by exactly two admins simultaneously from Normal to Emergency
  some s1, s2: State |
    s1.mode = Normal && s2.mode = Emergency && s1.time < s2.time &&
    some ete: EmergencyTriggerEvent |
      ete.occursAt = s2.time &&
      #ete.triggers = 2 &&
      all a: ete.triggers | a in Administrator
}

pred R1R2 {
  // R1 + R2: Emergency Triggering + Emergency mode duration and recovery to Normal mode
  R1 &&
  all s: State |
    s.mode = Emergency =>
      some s2: State | s2.mode = Normal && s2.time > s.time
}

pred R1R2R3 {
  // R1 + R2 + R3: Emergency Triggering, Duration, and Emergency Mode Behavior

  R1R2 &&

  // R3.1: Emergency mode request processing priority and concurrency
  all s: State |
    s.mode = Emergency =>
      // Requests processed in priority order High > Medium > Low
      all r1, r2: Request |
        r1 in s.processedRequests && r2 in s.processedRequests &&
        let c1 = requestClassification[r1], c2 = requestClassification[r2] |
          clearanceLess[c2, c1] =>
          r2.processedAt > r1.processedAt

  &&

  // R3.2: Mutual exclusivity lifted during Emergency
  all s: State, u: User |
    s.mode = Emergency =>
      no mep: MutualExclusivePair |
        mep.r1 in u.assignedRoles + s.delegatedRolesAt[u] &&
        mep.r2 in u.assignedRoles + s.delegatedRolesAt[u]

  &&

  // R3.3: Emergency privileges granted to admins during Emergency
  all s: State |
    s.mode = Emergency =>
      s.emergencyPrivilegesAt = (Administrator & s.users).permissions & EmergencyPrivilege
}

pred R1R2R3R4 {
  // R1 to R4: All requirements including Post-Emergency actions

  R1R2R3 &&

  // R4.1: Emergency privileges revoked after Emergency ends
  all s1, s2: State |
    s1.mode = Emergency && s2.mode = Normal && s1.time < s2.time =>
      no p: EmergencyPrivilege | p in s2.emergencyPrivilegesAt

  &&

  // R4.2: Delegated roles granted during Emergency revoked after Emergency ends
  all s1, s2: State |
    s1.mode = Emergency && s2.mode = Normal && s1.time < s2.time =>
      all dr: DelegatedRole |
        (dr.delegatedAt in (s1.time..s2.time)) =>
          dr not in s2.activeDelegations

  &&

  // R4.3: Admins update credentials after Emergency ends
  all s: State |
    s.mode = Normal =>
      all a: Administrator & s.users |
        some t: Time | t > s.time && t in a.updatedCredentialsAfterEmergency
}

pred All_Requirements {
  R1R2R3R4
}

///////////////////////////
// Assertions to check //
///////////////////////////

assert assertR1 {
  R1
}

assert assertR2 {
  R1R2
}

assert assertR3 {
  R1R2R3
}

assert assertR4 {
  R1R2R3R4
}

assert assertAll {
  All_Requirements
}

///////////////////////////
// Commands for quick checks //
///////////////////////////

run R1 for 5 but 3 User, 2 Administrator, 4 Time, 5 Request, 4 Role, 4 Permission, 3 DelegatedRole, 5 SysObject, 2 MutualExclusivePair
run R1R2 for 5 but 3 User, 2 Administrator, 6 Time, 5 Request, 4 Role, 4 Permission, 3 DelegatedRole, 5 SysObject, 2 MutualExclusivePair
run R1R2R3 for 7 but 4 User, 2 Administrator, 8 Time, 7 Request, 5 Role, 5 Permission, 4 DelegatedRole, 6 SysObject, 3 MutualExclusivePair
run R1R2R3R4 for 8 but 4 User, 2 Administrator, 10 Time, 7 Request, 5 Role, 5 Permission, 5 DelegatedRole, 6 SysObject, 3 MutualExclusivePair
run All_Requirements for 10 but 5 User, 3 Administrator, 12 Time, 8 Request, 6 Role, 6 Permission, 6 DelegatedRole, 7 SysObject, 4 MutualExclusivePair

check assertR1 for 5
check assertR2 for 6
check assertR3 for 8
check assertR4 for 10
check assertAll for 12
```

**[LESSON]:** Modeling temporal simultaneity as a concurrency event enables a clean abstraction of "simultaneous" triggers without concrete time intervals. Using separate SystemMode states indexed by Time allows temporal reasoning about mode transitions and properties. Abstracting emergency privileges as distinct Permission subsets clarifies privilege grants and revocation. Temporarily relaxing constraints (mutual exclusivity, FIFO ordering) during Emergency is effectively modeled by conditional facts based on mode. Delegated roles with delegation time and expiry allow temporal scoping and revocation modeling. Assertions and incremental predicates enable stepwise verification of requirements.

