SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) system for a multi-user environment supporting role hierarchy, temporary delegation, revocation, and concurrent request processing. Users have clearance levels (High, Medium, Low) restricting permissions and delegation. Requests affect system objects classified by clearance; users can submit requests only if clearance suffices. Higher-clearance users approve/reject requests from lower-clearance users. Requests are processed concurrently in FIFO order with liveness guarantees. Delegated roles expire or can be revoked. An Emergency Module adds special mode, privileges, and altered behavior.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
===========================================
- Users have clearance levels: High, Medium, Low.
- Administrators are a subset of High-clearance users.
- Users hold assigned and delegated roles; delegation constrained by clearance.
- Requests affect system objects classified by clearance.
- Request classification is highest classification of affected objects.
- Users submit requests only if request classification ≤ user clearance.
- Higher-clearance users can approve/reject requests from lower-clearance users.
- Requests processed concurrently in FIFO order during Normal mode.
- Every request eventually processed.
- Delegated roles expire after fixed time or revocation.
- Authorization correctness, delegation consistency, liveness, and concurrency safety preserved.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
===================================
R1: Emergency Triggering  
Two administrators must concurrently trigger emergency (within abstract concurrency event) during Normal mode to enter Emergency mode.

R2: Emergency Mode Duration and Recovery  
Emergency mode duration is fixed but can be ended earlier manually by internal control; system must eventually return to Normal mode.

R3: Emergency Mode Behavior  
R3.1: Request Processing Priority  
Emergency mode request processing prioritizes classification High > Medium > Low, with concurrency within each class.

R3.2: Mutual Exclusivity of Roles  
Mutual exclusivity constraints on roles enforced only during Normal mode; fully lifted during Emergency mode, allowing any mutually exclusive role pairs simultaneously.

R3.3: Emergency Privileges for Administrators  
Administrators hold additional emergency privileges (distinct permissions) only during Emergency mode.

R4: Post-Emergency Actions  
R4.1: Emergency Privileges Revocation  
Emergency privileges revoked immediately after Emergency mode ends.

R4.2: Delegated Roles Revocation  
Delegated roles granted during Emergency mode revoked immediately after mode ends; roles delegated before Emergency persist unless expired or revoked.

R4.3: Administrator Credential Update  
Administrators update credentials after Emergency mode ends.

KEY CONCEPTS AND ENTITIES
=========================
- User: Entity with clearance, assigned and delegated roles, submits requests.
- Administrator: High-clearance user with special privileges.
- Clearance Level: High, Medium, Low.
- Role: Set of permissions with minimum clearance.
- Delegated Role: Temporarily granted role with delegation metadata.
- Request: User-submitted action affecting system objects.
- System Object: Entity affected by requests, with classification.
- Request Classification: Highest classification among affected objects.
- Emergency Mode: Special system mode triggered by two admins concurrently.
- Emergency Privileges: Distinct permissions granted only during Emergency mode.
- Mutual Exclusive Roles: Role pairs mutually exclusive during Normal mode only.

CONSTRAINTS
===========
- Clearance levels ordered High > Medium > Low.
- Requests can be submitted only if request classification ≤ user clearance.
- Delegation requires delegate meets role minimum clearance; delegator has higher clearance than delegate.
- Delegated roles expire or may be revoked.
- Requests processed concurrently in FIFO order during Normal mode.
- Emergency mode entered only by exactly two admins triggering concurrently.
- Emergency mode duration fixed, may end early manually, eventually returns to Normal.
- Request processing priority in Emergency mode: High > Medium > Low with concurrency within classes.
- Mutual exclusivity enforced only during Normal mode.
- Emergency privileges granted only to admins during Emergency mode, revoked after.
- Delegated roles granted during Emergency revoked immediately after Emergency ends.
- Admins update credentials after Emergency ends.
