UPDATED REQUIREMENTS DOCUMENT

SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-based Access Control (RBAC) system operating in a multi-user environment with support for role hierarchy, temporary delegation of roles, revocation of delegated roles, and concurrent request processing. Users have security clearance levels (High, Medium, Low) which constrain their permissions and delegation capabilities. Requests affecting system objects are classified by the highest classification of affected objects, and can only be submitted if the user's clearance permits. Higher-clearance users can approve or reject lower-clearance users' requests. Requests are processed concurrently but normally in FIFO order, with guaranteed eventual processing. Temporary role delegation is allowed from higher-clearance users to lower-clearance users if minimum clearance requirements are met. Delegated roles expire after a fixed time or can be revoked anytime by administrators.

A new Emergency Module is to be integrated, adding functionality to handle emergency scenarios with special modes, privileges, and behavior changes.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
==========================================
- Users have one of three clearance levels: High, Medium, or Low.
- Administrators are users with High clearance, but not all High-clearance users are administrators.
- Users may hold assigned roles and delegated roles; delegation is constrained by clearance.
- Requests affect one or more system objects with classification levels (High, Medium, Low).
- Request classification is the highest classification level of any affected object.
- Users may only submit requests with classification ≤ user clearance.
- Higher-clearance users can approve or reject requests from lower-clearance users.
- Requests are processed concurrently in FIFO order during Normal mode.
- Every request is eventually processed (liveness).
- Role delegation is temporary and may be revoked by administrators.
- Delegated roles expire after a fixed time or may be revoked by administrators.
- The system preserves authorization correctness, delegation and permission consistency, liveness of request processing, and safety under concurrency.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
====================================
R1: Emergency Triggering  
While in Normal mode, exactly two administrators must trigger an emergency concurrently (within a defined short concurrency window abstracted as concurrency events) to cause the system to enter Emergency mode. Any pair of administrators may trigger the emergency.

R2: Emergency Mode Duration and Recovery  
Emergency mode duration is fixed but can be ended earlier manually by internal system control. The system must eventually return to Normal mode after Emergency mode ends.

R3: Emergency Mode Behavior  
R3.1: Request Processing Priority  
During Emergency mode, request processing may bypass FIFO ordering to prioritize processing requests by classification level in order High > Medium > Low. Concurrency applies to all classes; all High-classification requests are processed concurrently first, then all Medium, then all Low.

R3.2: Mutual Exclusivity of Roles  
During Emergency mode, mutual exclusivity constraints on roles that apply during Normal mode are fully lifted. Users may temporarily hold any pair of roles that are mutually exclusive under normal operation. Mutual exclusivity constraints remain strictly enforced only during Normal mode.

R3.3: Emergency Privileges for Administrators  
Administrators hold additional emergency privileges abstracted as distinct permissions temporarily while in Emergency mode only.

R4: Post-Emergency Actions  
R4.1: Revocation of Emergency Privileges  
When Emergency mode ends, emergency privileges must be revoked from administrators immediately.

R4.2: Revocation of Delegated Roles  
Delegated roles granted during Emergency mode must be revoked immediately after the mode ends. Delegated roles granted prior to Emergency mode persist unless expired or explicitly revoked.

R4.3: Administrator Credential Update  
Administrators must update their credentials after Emergency mode ends.

KEY CONCEPTS AND ENTITIES
==========================
- User: An entity with a clearance level who holds assigned and delegated roles and can submit requests.
- Administrator: A High-clearance user with special privileges and authority.
- Clearance Level: One of {High, Medium, Low} assigned to users and system objects.
- Role: A set of permissions; roles may have minimum clearance requirements.
- Delegated Role: A role temporarily granted to a user by a higher-clearance user.
- Request: An action submitted by a user affecting one or more system objects.
- System Object: An entity affected by requests, classified by clearance level.
- Request Classification: The highest classification of all affected objects in a request.
- Emergency Mode: A special system mode triggered by two administrators with altered processing rules and privileges.
- Emergency Privileges: Distinct permissions granted only to administrators during Emergency mode and revoked after.
- Mutual Exclusive Roles (during Emergency mode): Pairs of roles that are normally mutually exclusive but may be held simultaneously during Emergency mode.

ASSUMPTIONS REQUIRING CLARIFICATION
====================================
1. "Exactly two administrators" triggering emergency means any pair of two administrators triggering within a defined short concurrency window abstracted as concurrency events suffices to enter Emergency mode.

2. Mutual exclusivity constraints on roles apply only during Normal mode and are completely lifted during Emergency mode, allowing any mutually exclusive pairs of roles to be held temporarily.

3. Emergency privileges are distinct permissions granted exclusively to administrators during Emergency mode and revoked immediately after.

4. Delegated role revocation after Emergency mode applies exclusively to roles delegated during Emergency. Delegated roles granted before Emergency persist during Emergency unless expired or explicitly revoked.

5. Emergency mode duration is fixed but can be ended earlier manually by internal system control; transitions are internally managed with constraints ensuring eventual return to Normal mode.

6. Request processing priority ordering during Emergency mode is High > Medium > Low with concurrency applying to all classes; all requests of a given classification are processed concurrently before requests of lower classifications, overriding FIFO order.

CONSTRAINTS
===========
- User clearance levels and object classification levels are from the set {High, Medium, Low}, ordered High > Medium > Low.
- Users may submit requests only if request classification ≤ user clearance.
- Delegation of roles requires the delegate to meet the role’s minimum clearance.
- Delegated roles expire after a fixed time or may be revoked by administrators.
- Requests are processed concurrently in FIFO order during Normal mode.
- Every submitted request is eventually processed.
- Emergency mode can only be entered from Normal mode when exactly two administrators trigger simultaneously (any pair, within a short concurrency event window).
- Emergency mode duration is fixed and must eventually return to Normal mode but can be ended earlier manually.
- During Emergency mode, FIFO order may be bypassed to prioritize request processing by classification level: High > Medium > Low.
- Concurrency during Emergency mode applies to all request classes; all requests of a given classification are processed concurrently before processing requests of the next lower classification.
- Mutual exclusivity constraints on roles are strictly enforced only during Normal mode and are fully relaxed during Emergency mode.
- Emergency privileges are distinct permissions granted to administrators only during Emergency mode and revoked immediately afterwards.
- Delegated roles granted during Emergency mode are revoked immediately when Emergency mode ends; delegated roles granted before Emergency persist during Emergency unless expired or explicitly revoked.
- Administrators must update credentials after Emergency mode ends.

