SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) solution supporting multiple users with clearance levels (High, Medium, Low), role hierarchy, temporary role delegation with revocation, and concurrent request processing. Requests affect classified system objects and can be submitted only by users with sufficient clearance. Administrators (a subset of High-clearance users) can approve or reject requests submitted by lower-clearance users. The system processes requests concurrently in FIFO order during Normal mode and guarantees eventual processing. Delegated roles expire after fixed times or can be revoked. An Emergency Module adds a special system mode with altered behavior, privileges, and constraints.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
===========================================
- Users have clearance levels: High, Medium, Low.
- Administrators are a subset of High-clearance users.
- Users hold assigned and delegated roles; delegation constrained by clearance.
- Requests affect system objects classified by clearance.
- Requests can be submitted only if request classification ≤ user clearance.
- Higher-clearance users can approve or reject requests from lower-clearance users.
- Requests processed concurrently in FIFO order during Normal mode.
- All requests eventually processed (liveness).
- Delegated roles expire after fixed time or may be revoked.
- Authorization correctness, delegation consistency, liveness, and concurrency safety preserved.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
===================================
R1: Emergency Triggering  
Exactly two administrators must trigger emergency concurrently (within an abstract concurrency event) during Normal mode to enter Emergency mode.

R2: Emergency Mode Duration and Recovery  
Emergency mode duration is fixed but can be ended earlier manually by internal system control; system must eventually return to Normal mode.

R3: Emergency Mode Behavior  
R3.1: Request Processing Priority  
During Emergency mode, requests are processed with priority ordering High > Medium > Low; concurrency applies within each classification.

R3.2: Mutual Exclusivity of Roles  
Mutual exclusivity constraints on roles apply strictly during Normal mode only and are fully lifted during Emergency mode, allowing any mutually exclusive role pairs to be held simultaneously.

R3.3: Emergency Privileges for Administrators  
Administrators hold additional emergency privileges (distinct permissions) exclusively during Emergency mode.

R4: Post-Emergency Actions  
R4.1: Emergency Privileges Revocation  
Emergency privileges must be revoked immediately after Emergency mode ends.

R4.2: Delegated Roles Revocation  
Delegated roles granted during Emergency mode must be revoked immediately after mode ends; delegated roles granted before Emergency persist unless expired or revoked.

R4.3: Administrator Credential Update  
Administrators must update credentials after Emergency mode ends.
```

---

**[LESSON]:**  
- Conditional enforcement of mode-dependent constraints is crucial to prevent contradictory facts and vacuous verification outcomes.  
- Explicit logical connectors in Alloy predicates and facts are mandatory to avoid syntax errors, especially with multiple quantifications.  
- Adding existence assertions for scenarios exercising lifted constraints helps avoid vacuous passing.  
- Increasing scope parameters during runs enhances coverage of concurrency and delegation edge cases.  
- Scoping delegation revocation to roles delegated during Emergency prevents unintended revocation of pre-existing delegations.  
- Liveness and concurrency assertions strengthen behavioral guarantees such as eventual request processing and priority enforcement.

**[PATTERN]:**  
- Syntax errors often stem from missing logical connectors between expressions.  
- Vacuity in assertions lifting constraints can occur if no instance exercises the lifted scenario; existence assertions are necessary.  
- Mode-dependent constraints should be conditional, keyed on system mode, to avoid conflicts.  
- Incremental predicates aid in localizing failures and understanding requirement interactions.

